  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000088044,
      "SummaryText": "Accidental Release Prevention and Emergency Response\n\nThis site uses n-Pentane and Isobutane in its manufacturing operations. Handling systems were designed with controls to prevent and minimize accidental releases. Written unloading procedures require supervision of the unloading process by workers trained to detect failures and prevent releases. Alarms, excess flow devices and automatic shut down systems connected to detectors are employed. Our Emergency Response Plan includes response by employees trained in the handling of hazardous materials and by local fire and emergency response agencies. There is a strictly enforced no smoking or open flame policy in areas where pentane or Isobutane may be present.\n\nThe Stationary Source and Regulated Substances Handled\n\nThe site has several manufacturing areas; pentane is used in EPS bead production areas and Isobutane is used in foam sheet extrusion area.\n\nThe n-Pentane is used to manufacture expandable polystyrene (EPS) beads. It is usually received at the site in rail cars, stored in underground storage tanks and pumped to batch tanks used for the manufacture of EPS beads. The maximum amount of n-Pentane stored on site is 355,000 pounds in the entire EPS system.\n\nIsobutane is used in the extrusion of polystyrene foam sheet which is subsequently processed into various disposable food service items. The Isobutane is usually received on site in trucks and is stored in a 60,000 gallon above ground storage tank. The area has the capability to receive isobutane via railcars, but this practice isn't currently economical. The Isobutane is pumped to foam extruders where it is injected into melted polystyrene during the production of foam sheet. The maximum amount of Isobutane stored on site is 252,180 pounds.\n\nRelease Prevention Program\n\nThe facility maintains written procedures for the safe handling of pentane & isobutane and employees directly involved are trained in safe handling practices. Detectors are appropriately placed"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000088044,
      "SummaryText": "to alert staff to the presence of pentane & isobutane should a release occur.\n\nFive Year Accident History\n\nThere were no reportable incidents within the last five years.\n\nThe Emergency Response Program\n\nTrained first responders are available on site under the direction of the EHS Manager. The ERP is periodically updated and training drills are conducted for the emergency response team. We communicate with the local fire and emergency response services to ensure their understanding of the potential hazards at this facility.\n\nPlanned Changes To Improve Safety\n\nWe continue to update our emergency response plan, training and capabilities.\n\nSince COVID-19, the compliance audits was conducted in August of 2023 for the foam dept and in October 2024 for the bead department. A PHA will be conducted in the foam department before the end of 2025."
    }
  ]
